  summary: [
    "Sorting arranges elements by an order relation; searching locates a key or reports that it is absent.",
    "Internal sorts work in main memory; external sorts use secondary storage and merging.",
    "Insertion sort grows a sorted prefix and is Θ(n) on sorted input, Θ(n²) on average and in the worst case.",
    "Selection sort repeatedly places the minimum at the front and is Θ(n²) on every input, with at most n−1 swaps.",
    "Bubble sort swaps adjacent out-of-order pairs; it is Θ(n²) in general, and Θ(n) on sorted input with an early-exit flag.",
    "Linear search works on unsorted data in O(n) worst-case time.",
    "Binary search requires sorted data and runs in O(log n) worst-case time by halving the search space.",
    "Sorting once can make repeated searches cheaper, but the upfront O(n²) cost must be justified by reuse."
  ],
  keyTerms: [
    { term: "Internal sort", definition: "A sort that keeps all data in primary memory." },
    { term: "External sort", definition: "A sort that uses secondary storage and merges separately sorted runs." },
    { term: "Key", definition: "The value a search is looking for; the method returns its index or −1." },
    { term: "Pass", definition: "One complete scan of the unsorted portion of the array." },
    { term: "Adaptive", definition: "An algorithm whose running time improves on favorable input; insertion sort is adaptive." },
    { term: "Early exit", definition: "Stopping bubble sort when a pass makes no exchanges." },
    { term: "Bisection", definition: "Halving the search interval in binary search." },
    { term: "Precondition", definition: "A condition that must hold before an algorithm is valid; binary search requires sorted data." }
  ],
  next: "chapter-03"
